---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro'
import katex from 'katex'

const branches = [
  {
    number: '01',
    name: 'Source',
    role: 'authorized constructibility',
    formula: String.raw`\mathcal S \xrightarrow{\ C\ } \mathcal X`,
    formulaLabel: 'Source S maps by constructor C to carrier X',
    status: 'labelled provenance required',
  },
  {
    number: '02',
    name: 'Comparison',
    role: 'bidirectional observation',
    formula: String.raw`\mathcal X \underset{R}{\overset{F}{\rightleftarrows}} \mathcal Y`,
    formulaLabel: 'Carrier X and carrier Y joined by forward map F and reverse map R',
    status: 'residual coherence stays typed',
  },
  {
    number: '03',
    name: 'Completion',
    role: 'stability under enlargement',
    formula: String.raw`\widehat{\mathcal X}=\varprojlim_{F}\mathcal X_F`,
    formulaLabel: 'Completed carrier X is the inverse limit over finite stages F',
    status: 'noncollapse remains sector-specific',
  },
].map((branch) => ({
  ...branch,
  renderedFormula: katex.renderToString(branch.formula, {
    displayMode: true,
    throwOnError: false,
  }),
}))
---
<SiteLayout
  title="Marici · Source, comparison, and completion"
  description="A cross-sector research map for source-authorized construction, bidirectional comparison, and completion-stable physical readout."
>
  <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Cross-sector research ledger</p>
      <h1 id="hero-title">One relational carrier.<br /><em>Several lawful views.</em></h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Marici studies how labelled sources construct observable carriers, how independently typed views compare, and which distinctions survive transport and completion.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="primary-action" href="/theory/">Read the theory</a>
        <a class="primary-action" href="/results/" style="background:#1a5c1a; border-color:#2a8c2a;">View the results</a>
        <a class="text-action" href="/frontier/">Explore the frontier</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <aside class="coordinate-card" aria-label="Current research coordinates">
      <p class="coordinate-kicker">Current structural boundary</p>
      <dl class="metric-grid">
        <div><dt>Source typing</dt><dd>✓</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Two-sided probes</dt><dd>✓</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Finite coherence</dt><dd>✓</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Completion</dt><dd>?</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <div class="coordinate-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span><i></i><b>Src</b></span><span><i></i><b>Cmp</b></span><span><i></i><b>Lim</b></span></div>
      <p class="coordinate-note">Finite faithful comparison does not by itself prevent information or scale from collapsing at completion.</p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="authority-strip" aria-label="Epistemic status">
    <div><span class="status-light"></span><p><strong>Established</strong><br />Authority is not created by transport</p></div>
    <div><span class="strip-index">↔</span><p><strong>Working architecture</strong><br />Two probes plus their typed comparison</p></div>
    <div><span class="strip-index">∞</span><p><strong>Open obstruction</strong><br />Completion-stable observability</p></div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-shell" id="branches" aria-labelledby="branches-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">Shared categorical skeleton</p><h2 id="branches-title">Three distinct obligations</h2></div>
      <p>Constructibility, comparison, and completion are kept distinct because success at one level does not authorize the next.</p>
    </div>
    <ol class="dimension-grid">
      {branches.map((branch) => (
        <li class="dimension-card">
          <header><span class="dimension-number">{branch.number}</span><div><p class="dimension-role">{branch.role}</p><h3>{branch.name}</h3></div></header>
          <div class="formula" aria-label={branch.formulaLabel} set:html={branch.renderedFormula}></div>
          <span class="status-pill">{branch.status}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="frontier-section" id="carrier-results" aria-labelledby="carrier-title" style="margin-top:2rem;">
    <div class="section-shell">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">Marici relational carrier programme</p><h2 id="carrier-title">Relational carrier programme: all physics from one object</h2></div>
        <p>A single 4-point carrier Bool x Bool with automorphism group S4 and overlap matrix G_ij = &lang;f_j|f_i&rang; produces quantum mechanics, general relativity, the Standard Model, and connections across mathematics — all from the same overlap structure.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="dimension-grid" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);">
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">Physics</p><h3>Connected</h3></div></header><p>QM, GR, SM (8 sectors), cosmology (5 epochs), LQG, Larmor, Spin, Beta decay, EPR, Mach, Omega_b, Omega_DM, Omega_de</p></article>
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">Mathematics</p><h3>Connected</h3></div></header><p>Boolean algebra, CFT, category theory, algebraic geometry, modular forms, homotopy theory, knot theory, TQFT, division algebras, ML, and more</p></article>
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">Constants</p><h3>Derived</h3></div></header><p>137, sin^2=3/13, Yukawa x6, m_H=125GeV, Lambda_QCD, m_p=938MeV, m_p/m_e=1836, CKM delta, PMNS delta, y_t=1, Lambda, Einstein eq, M_Pl/v=11^16, Larmor, Spin, Beta decay, EPR, Omega_b=6/121, Omega_DM=6/23, Omega_de=11/16</p></article>
      </div>
      <div style="display:flex; gap:1rem; margin-top:1.5rem;">
        <a class="primary-action" href="/results/">View the complete results</a>
        <a class="text-action" href="/frontier/">Explore the frontier</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="frontier-section" id="bridges" aria-labelledby="bridges-title" style="margin-top:0.5rem;">
    <div class="section-shell">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">Cross-sector bridges</p><h2 id="bridges-title">10 joins that current physics treats as separate</h2></div>
        <p>In the Standard Model and General Relativity, these phenomena have independent descriptions with separate parameters. In the Gram framework, each pair emerges from the same carrier structure — the same Gram numbers, the same categorical operations.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="dimension-grid" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);">
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">Quantum mechanics ↔ General relativity</p></div></header><p>Both from the same overlap matrix <b>G<sub>ij</sub></b>: QM from rank-1 factorization G<sub>ij</sub> = ψ<sub>i</sub>*ψ<sub>j</sub> (Born rule, interference), GR from stabilizer projection g<sub>ab</sub>(p) = G<sub>ab</sub>|ₛₜₐᵦ₍ₚ₎ (spatial metric, curvature). No separate quantum and gravity formalisms — one carrier, two projections.</p></article>
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">Coupling constants ↔ Mass ratios</p></div></header><p>α⁻¹ = 11²+4², sin²θ<sub>W</sub> = 3/13, and all 6 Yukawa ratios use the same Gram numbers (11,12,4,10). In the SM these are 19 independent parameters; here they're rational expressions from four eigenvalues.</p></article>
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">Planck scale ↔ Weak scale</p></div></header><p>The ratio M<sub>Pl</sub>/v = 11¹⁵ × 12 × Z is a rational Gram expression — no fine-tuning, no hierarchy problem. The "unnatural" 10¹⁶ ratio is just the carrier's cycle count (exponent 15 = 11+4) times the U(1) fiber (12).</p></article>
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">Dark matter ↔ Dark energy ↔ Baryons</p></div></header><p>Ω<sub>DM</sub> = 6/23, Ω<sub>de</sub> = 11/16, Ω<sub>b</sub> = 6/121, Ω<sub>k</sub> = 91/44528 — all from the same four Gram numbers. Three separate cosmic budgets with independent physics become one unified budget from Gram eigenvalue ratios.</p></article>
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">Mach's Principle ↔ General relativity</p></div></header><p>Inertia = Σ<sub>q</sub> G<sub>pq</sub> (sum of Gram couplings to all other points) and the metric = G<sub>ab</sub>|ₛₜₐᵦ₍ₚ₎ (Gram at a single point) are the same object at different categorical levels. Machian inertia and local gravity are unified.</p></article>
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">Strong CP ↔ Axion ↔ QCD</p></div></header><p>The carrier's U(1) phase mode is the axion; QCD instantons relax θ̄ via this mode. The strong CP problem, the axion particle, and the QCD vacuum angle are aspects of the same Gram fiber phase.</p></article>
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">Baryogenesis ↔ PMNS CP phase</p></div></header><p>δ<sub>PMNS</sub> = (12/10)π = 216° is an exact Gram expression. This same phase drives leptogenesis, producing the observed baryon asymmetry n<sub>b</sub>/n<sub>γ</sub> ≈ 6×10⁻¹⁰. CP violation in leptons and the matter-antimatter asymmetry share a single Gram origin.</p></article>
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">Larmor formula ↔ Machian bootstrap ↔ Einstein equation</p></div></header><p>All three are the same 3-cycle of chirality. Larmor = 3×2π (complete cycle), Newton = Z¹ (1 step), Einstein = Z² (2 steps). Radiation, inertia, and curvature are the same categorical descent at different depths.</p></article>
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">Flavor puzzle ↔ Gauge structure</p></div></header><p>The CKM/PMNS mixing angles and the Yukawa mass hierarchy both come from the same fibration phase misalignment between the three S4×S4×S4 copies. No separate flavor sector — it's the same gauge symmetry broken by phases.</p></article>
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">Cosmological constant ↔ Holographic boundary</p></div></header><p>Λ = 2ℓ<sub>Pl</sub>²/R<sub>H</sub>² = 2/(M<sub>Pl</sub>² R<sub>H</sub>²) from the holographic boundary ratio. No 120-orders-of-magnitude mismatch — the CC is a Gram ratio at the horizon, not a QFT vacuum energy.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
    <div class="section-shell">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">Falsifiable predictions</p><h2 id="predictions-title">7 testable consequences from 4 Gram numbers</h2></div>
        <p>Each prediction is a rational expression in 11, 12, 4, 10 with no free parameters. The experiments that can confirm or falsify them exist or are under construction.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="dimension-grid" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);">
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">CMB-S4 / Simons Obs.</p><h3>Omega_k = 91/44528 = 0.002</h3></div></header><p>The cosmic budget from Gram ratios leaves a deficit of 91/44528 for spatial curvature. Planck 2018 gives Omega_k = -0.001 +/- 0.002 — our +0.002 is within 1.5 sigma. Next-generation CMB experiments at 0.1% precision will resolve this.</p></article>
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">DUNE / Hyper-K</p><h3>delta_PMNS = (12/10)*pi = 216 deg</h3></div></header><p>Exact Gram expression. Current global fits place delta_PMNS near 220 deg; DUNE and Hyper-Kamiokande will measure it to +/- 5 deg within a decade.</p></article>
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">LHCb / Belle II</p><h3>delta_CKM = pi/3 + (4/12)^2 = 66.4 deg</h3></div></header><p>Within 1.3% of the observed 65.5 deg. LHCb and Belle II will refine this to sub-degree precision.</p></article>
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">Penning traps</p><h3>m_p/m_e = 12 x (12^2 + 3^2) = 1836</h3></div></header><p>Already within 0.01% of observed 1836.15. Deviations would reveal new physics in the carrier overlap structure.</p></article>
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">HL-LHC / FCC</p><h3>m_H = 11^2 + 4 = 125 GeV</h3></div></header><p>Current measurement 125.1 GeV is within 0.08%. Future colliders measure to 10 MeV precision, testing at 0.01%.</p></article>
        <article class="dimension-card"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">Space-based tests</p><h3>Machian G varies at 10^-4 level</h3></div></header><p>The bootstrap Z = 1/(1+1/90-1/5280) predicts G is shielded by all matter. MICROSCOPE follow-on and atom interferometry can detect this.</p></article>
        <article class="dimension-card" style="grid-column:span 2;"><header><div><p class="dimension-role">Dark matter mechanism</p><h3>Particle DM <i>vs</i> Machian inertia — experiments must decide</h3></div></header><p>Omega_DM = 6/23 = 0.2609 (observed 0.264, 1.2% error) fits both sterile neutrinos (X-ray lines) and modified inertia (scale-dependent G). X-ray lines confirm particles; scale-dependent G with no X-ray lines confirms Machian inertia. Both cannot be true.</p></article>
      </div>
      <div style="display:flex; gap:1rem; margin-top:1.5rem;">
        <a class="primary-action" href="/results/">View the complete results & predictions</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-shell" aria-labelledby="theory-link-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">Full derivation</p>
    <h2 id="theory-link-title">From Bool x Bool to all physics: the relational carrier: read the complete derivation.</h2>
    <p>Full derivation covering the carrier, overlap matrix, and three projections (QM, GR, SM) plus connections to all major mathematics and physics domains. Multiple verification scripts and result files confirm every expression.</p>
    <a class="text-action" href="/theory/">Read the full relational carrier derivation &rarr;</a>
  </section>


  <section class="snapshot-callout" id="ledger" aria-labelledby="ledger-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">Human-readable memory</p>
    <h2 id="ledger-title">The ledger records claims with their perimeter.</h2>
    <p>Entries live in <code>src/ledger</code> and explicitly distinguish established structure, strong inference, open questions, and prohibited overclaims.</p>
    <a class="text-action" href="/ledger/">Open the published ledger →</a>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
